from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q001-060')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e,track='Mathematics'):w.add(n,track,topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(22,'Number Theory',r'''Let A={p/q∈(0,1):p∈ℕ, q=2ⁿ for n∈ℕ∪{0}, gcd(p,q)=1}, B={p/q∈(0,1):p∈ℕ, q=2ⁿ5ᵐ for n,m∈ℕ∪{0}, gcd(p,q)=1}, and C={p/q∈(0,1):p/q has a terminating decimal expansion}. Which statement is true?''',[r'A⊊C and B⊊C',r'A⊊C⊊B',r'A⊊B⊊C',r'A⊊B=C'],'D',['A terminating decimal is an integer divided by a power of 10.','For a reduced fraction, its denominator must divide some 10ᵏ.'],r'''If p/q in lowest terms terminates after k decimal places, p/q=a/10ᵏ; coprimality forces q|10ᵏ. Thus q has only prime factors 2 and 5. Conversely if q=2ⁿ5ᵐ, choose k≥max(n,m); then q|10ᵏ and p/q has a terminating decimal. Hence B=C. Every dyadic rational in A lies in B. The inclusion is strict since 1/5∈B but 1/5∉A. Therefore A⊊B=C.''',r'''A rational number is in lowest terms when its numerator and denominator are coprime. A terminating base-b expansion means that the number equals an integer divided by bᵏ for some nonnegative integer k. For p/q in lowest terms, this is equivalent to q dividing bᵏ: one direction follows by cross-multiplication and Euclid's divisibility lemma, and the other by multiplying numerator and denominator to obtain denominator bᵏ. Unique prime factorization therefore says that only primes dividing b may divide q. In base ten these primes are 2 and 5; dyadic rationals use only 2. To prove strict inclusion, establish ordinary inclusion first and exhibit one element of the larger set outside the smaller. An unreduced denominator can be misleading, so cancellation must precede the prime-factor test.''',[('Does 7/40 have a terminating decimal?','40=2³·5, so it divides 10³. Multiplying numerator and denominator by 25 gives 175/1000=.175.'),('Which of 1/3 and 1/9 terminate in base 12?','12 has prime factors 2,3. Both denominators use only 3; 3 divides 12 and 9 divides 12². In fact 1/3=4/12 and 1/9=16/144.')])
add(23,'Discrete Mathematics',r'''Let A,B be nonempty subsets of ℕ with |A|≥2. Let S₁={f:A→B:f is one-to-one} and S₂={g:B→A:g is onto}. Which statement is true?''',[r'If A⊊B and B is finite, there is a one-to-one map S₂→S₁.',r'If B=ℕ, there is a one-to-one map S₂→B.',r'If B=ℕ and A is finite, there is a one-to-one map B→S₁.',r'If A is finite, S₂ is finite for any B.'],'C',['For finite A and B=ℕ, construct infinitely many distinct injections A→ℕ.','Test the other claims using A={1,2}, either B={1,2,3,4} or B=ℕ.'],r'''For C enumerate A={a₁,…,aₖ}, and for n∈ℕ define fₙ(aᵢ)=kn+i (i=1,…,k). Each fₙ is injective and different n give different maps, yielding an injection ℕ→S₁. A is false: for A={1,2}, B={1,2,3,4}, |S₁|=4·3=12, but |S₂|=2⁴−2=14. For B=ℕ,A={1,2}, there are uncountably many surjections: each nonempty proper subset of ℕ gives a distinct map by assigning 1 on that subset and 2 elsewhere. Hence no injection S₂→ℕ exists, and S₂ is not finite. This disproves B and D.''',r'''An injective map sends different domain elements to different images, whereas a surjective map attains every codomain element. For finite sets with sizes k and m, the number of injections from a k-element set to an m-element set is m(m−1)⋯(m−k+1), with zero possibilities if k>m. Onto maps from m points to two points correspond to binary assignments except the two constant ones, giving 2ᵐ−2. For infinite domains even a finite codomain of size at least two can yield uncountably many functions: binary-valued functions correspond to subsets, and Cantor's theorem makes the power set of ℕ uncountable. To establish an injection between function spaces, construct distinct indexed functions explicitly. Do not infer finite cardinality of a function space from finite cardinality of its codomain.''',[('Count injections from a two-element set to a five-element set.','The first image has 5 choices and the second 4, so there are 20 injections.'),('Count surjections from a four-element set onto a two-element set.','There are 2⁴=16 functions. Remove the two constant functions that omit one target; 14 remain.')])
add(24,'Real Analysis',r'''Let f:ℝ\ℚ→ℝ\ℚ be f(x)=(3x+2)/(4x+3). Let x₁ be irrational and xₙ₊₁=f(xₙ). Suppose xₙ converges to a real ℓ. Which is true?''',[r'If ℓ is positive, ℓ=√3/2.',r'If ℓ is positive, ℓ=1/√2.',r'If ℓ is negative, ℓ=−√3/2.',r'If ℓ is negative, ℓ=−1/2.'],'B',['Use the recurrence after cross-multiplication to avoid a potential limit at the pole.','Passing to the limit gives ℓ(4ℓ+3)=3ℓ+2.'],r'''Each irrational xₙ avoids −3/4, so xₙ₊₁(4xₙ+3)=3xₙ+2. Both xₙ and xₙ₊₁ tend to ℓ; taking limits yields 4ℓ²+3ℓ=3ℓ+2, or ℓ²=1/2. Therefore the positive possible limit is 1/√2 and the negative is −1/√2. Both are irrational fixed points, so starting at either realizes its limit; the negative alternatives are not valid. The Möbius map preserves irrationality because solving a rational output y for x gives x=(2−3y)/(4y−3), rational when possible.''',r'''A convergent iterative sequence xₙ₊₁=f(xₙ) often has a fixed-point limit: if f is continuous at the limit ℓ, then ℓ=f(ℓ). Rational functions are continuous where their denominators are nonzero, but that condition must be checked rather than assumed. Cross-multiplying the recurrence before taking limits gives a polynomial identity valid for every term and can exclude a pole without invoking continuity there. The shifted sequence xₙ₊₁ has the same limit as xₙ. A fixed-point equation supplies necessary possible limits; it does not by itself prove convergence from all starting values. To refute an asserted implication concerning a sign, starting at an actual fixed point supplies a convergent counterexample. For a nondegenerate Möbius map with rational coefficients, an irrational input cannot have a rational output because the inverse equation would force a rational input.''',[('If xₙ₊₁=(2xₙ+3)/(xₙ+2) converges, find possible limits.','Cross-multiplication and limits give ℓ(ℓ+2)=2ℓ+3, hence ℓ²=3. Possible limits are ±√3; starting at each fixed point realizes it.'),('If xₙ₊₁=(xₙ+2)/3 and the sequence converges, find its limit.','Continuity gives ℓ=(ℓ+2)/3, so ℓ=1. In fact xₙ−1=(x₁−1)/3ⁿ⁻¹ proves convergence for every starting value.')])
add(25,'Real Analysis',r'f(x)=x logₑ(1+1/x), x∈(0,∞). Which is true?',['f is unbounded.','f is increasing.',r'lim(x→∞)f(x)=2.','f is decreasing.'],'B',['Differentiate and set t=1/x.','Show log(1+t)>t/(1+t) for t>0 by differentiating their difference.'],r'''f′(x)=log(1+1/x)−1/(x+1). Set t=1/x>0 and g(t)=log(1+t)−t/(1+t). Then g(0)=0 and g′(t)=t/(1+t)²>0, so f′(x)>0. Also log(1+t)<t for t>0, hence 0<f(x)<1, and log(1+t)/t→1 as t→0 shows f(x)→1 as x→∞. Thus only the increasing assertion is true.''',r'''A differentiable real function on an interval is increasing if its derivative is everywhere nonnegative, and strictly increasing when its derivative is positive. Logarithmic inequalities can be proved by introducing their difference and examining its derivative, rather than relying on a power series outside its range of validity. For t>0, integration of 1/(1+s) over [0,t] gives t/(1+t)<log(1+t)<t, since the integrand lies strictly between those endpoint values except at endpoints. Substitution t=1/x turns a limit at infinity into one at zero. The fundamental logarithmic limit log(1+t)/t→1 follows from the derivative of log at 1 or these bounds. Monotonicity and boundedness are independent properties; increasing functions on unbounded intervals need not be unbounded.''',[('Show h(x)=x/(x+1) increases for x>0 and find its limit.','h′(x)=1/(x+1)²>0. Since h=1−1/(x+1), its limit at infinity is 1 and it is bounded by 1.'),('Prove log(1+t)<t for t>0.','Let k(t)=t−log(1+t). Then k(0)=0 and k′(t)=t/(1+t)>0, hence k(t)>0.')])
add(26,'Real Analysis',r'''For n≥1 define fₙ:[0,1]→ℝ by fₙ(x)=nx on [0,1/n], 2−nx on (1/n,2/n], and 0 on (2/n,1]. Which is true?''',[r'fₙ converges uniformly on [0,1] to a continuous f.',r'fₙ converges pointwise to a discontinuous f.',r'fₙ converges pointwise to a continuous f.',r'fₙ does not converge pointwise on [0,1].'],'C',['Fix x before letting n tend to infinity.','For x>0 eventually 2/n<x; then compare with the moving peak x=1/n.'],'At x=0, fₙ(0)=0. For each fixed x>0 choose n>2/x; then x>2/n and fₙ(x)=0. Thus fₙ→f≡0 pointwise, and the limit is continuous. Yet fₙ(1/n)=1 for every n, so sup|fₙ−f|=1 and convergence is not uniform. Therefore C is true. For small n each branch is understood restricted to the declared domain [0,1].',r'''Pointwise convergence means that for each fixed x and each ε>0 there is an index N that may depend on x, after which |fₙ(x)−f(x)|<ε. Uniform convergence requires one N that works for every x in the domain; equivalently the supremum norm of the error tends to zero. A narrow moving spike may eventually vanish at every fixed location while retaining a constant maximum height, producing pointwise but nonuniform convergence. The uniform limit theorem says that a uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous, but its converse is false: a continuous pointwise limit does not establish uniform convergence. Always determine the pointwise limit first, then analyze the supremum of the error, permitting the maximizing point to depend on n.''',[('Let gₙ(x)=xⁿ on [0,1]. Find its pointwise limit and test uniform convergence.','For x<1, xⁿ→0; at 1 it stays 1. The discontinuous limit cannot be a uniform limit of continuous gₙ, so convergence is not uniform.'),('Let hₙ(x)=x/n on [0,1]. Is convergence to zero uniform?','The maximum error is sup₀≤x≤₁ x/n=1/n→0. Thus convergence is uniform and the limit is continuous.')])
add(27,'Linear Algebra',r'Which polynomial is the characteristic polynomial of a real 2×2 matrix A with trace(A)=7 and trace(A²)=29?',[r't²+7t+10',r't²−7t+29',r't²−7t−10',r't²−7t+10'],'D',['For a 2×2 matrix the polynomial is t²−trace(A)t+det(A).','Use trace(A²)=trace(A)²−2det(A).'],'For A=[[a,b],[c,d]], trace(A²)=a²+d²+2bc=(a+d)²−2(ad−bc). Thus 29=49−2det(A), giving det(A)=10. The characteristic polynomial det(tI−A) is t²−7t+10. It is realized, for example, by diag(2,5).',r'''The characteristic polynomial of an n×n matrix A is det(tI−A); its roots over an algebraic closure are eigenvalues counted with algebraic multiplicity. For a 2×2 matrix it is t²−tr(A)t+det(A). Trace is the sum of diagonal entries and also the sum of eigenvalues, while determinant is their product. Direct expansion yields tr(A²)=(tr A)²−2det A, even when the matrix is not diagonalizable. This identity is a special Newton relation between power sums and elementary symmetric functions. The Cayley–Hamilton theorem states that A satisfies its characteristic polynomial. In problems involving traces, recover polynomial coefficients using these invariant identities. Do not replace tr(A²) by (tr A)²; their difference encodes the determinant.''',[('Find the characteristic polynomial when tr A=5 and tr A²=13 for a 2×2 matrix.','13=25−2det A gives det A=6. Hence the polynomial is t²−5t+6=(t−2)(t−3).'),('A has eigenvalues 1 and 4. Compute tr A² and its characteristic polynomial.','The squared eigenvalues sum to 1+16=17. Trace is 5, determinant 4, and the polynomial is t²−5t+4.')])
add(28,'Linear Algebra',r'Let 𝔽₅ be the field with five elements. How many 2×2 matrices with entries in 𝔽₅ have rank one?',['125','144','145','480'],'B',['Write a nonzero rank-one matrix as uvᵀ with u,v nonzero.','Two pairs give the same matrix precisely by reciprocal nonzero scalar rescaling.'],r'''Each nonzero rank-one matrix equals uvᵀ with u,v∈𝔽₅²\{0}. There are (5²−1)²=24² such pairs. The same matrix has exactly 5−1=4 representations, namely (cu,c⁻¹v) for c≠0. Hence the count is 576/4=144. Alternatively there are (25−1)/(5−1)=6 possible image lines, and for each line 25−1=24 nonzero maps into it.''',r'''The rank of a matrix is the dimension of its column space, or equivalently of its image as a linear transformation. A rank-one matrix over a field has the form uvᵀ with both vectors nonzero, because all columns are scalar multiples of one fixed nonzero column. This representation is not unique: multiplying u by a nonzero scalar and v by its inverse preserves the product. Over a finite field of q elements there are qᵐ−1 nonzero m-vectors, and each one-dimensional subspace contains q−1 nonzero vectors. Consequently the number of rank-one m×n matrices is (qᵐ−1)(qⁿ−1)/(q−1). This formula excludes the zero matrix, whose rank is zero. The finite-field assumption matters: counting scalar representatives without dividing by q−1 overcounts each matrix.''',[('How many rank-one 2×2 matrices over 𝔽₂ are there?','There are 3 choices each for u and v, and only one nonzero scalar. Hence 3·3/1=9.'),('Count rank-one 2×3 matrices over 𝔽₃.','The formula gives (3²−1)(3³−1)/(3−1)=8·26/2=104.')])
add(29,'Linear Algebra',r'''X is the real vector space of twice differentiable real functions on [0,1]. Define φ:X→ℝ³ by φ(f)=(f(1),f′(1),f″(1)). Which is true?''',[r'dim(X/ker φ)=3.',r'ker φ is finite dimensional.',r'dim(X/ker φ)=1.',r'X is finite dimensional.'],'A',['Construct a quadratic with any prescribed value and first two derivatives at 1.','Apply the first isomorphism theorem; powers (x−1)ᵏ for k≥3 lie in the kernel.'],r'''For any (a,b,c)∈ℝ³, f(x)=a+b(x−1)+(c/2)(x−1)² has φ(f)=(a,b,c), so φ is onto. The first isomorphism theorem gives X/ker φ≅im φ=ℝ³, hence dimension 3. The functions (x−1)ᵏ, k≥3, lie in ker φ and are linearly independent, proving the kernel and X are infinite dimensional.''',r'''For a linear map T:V→W, its kernel consists of vectors sent to zero and its image consists of all attained outputs. The quotient V/ker T identifies vectors whose difference lies in the kernel. The first isomorphism theorem gives V/ker T≅im T, regardless of whether V is finite dimensional; therefore a finite-dimensional image yields a finite-dimensional quotient even when the original space and kernel are infinite dimensional. Evaluation of a function and its derivatives at a fixed point is linear. Polynomials in powers of x−a provide prescribed derivative data through their Taylor coefficients, and sufficiently high powers annihilate all low-order derivative evaluations. Distinct monomials are linearly independent because a zero polynomial has every coefficient zero. Avoid applying finite-dimensional subtraction to two infinite dimensions.''',[('On polynomials define T(p)=(p(0),p′(0)). Find quotient dimension.','The polynomial a+bx attains any (a,b), so T is onto ℝ². The quotient by its kernel is isomorphic to ℝ² and has dimension 2; the kernel contains all powers xᵏ for k≥2.'),('Let T:ℝ⁴→ℝ² be T(a,b,c,d)=(a+c,b+d). Find kernel dimension.','T is onto. Kernel vectors satisfy c=−a,d=−b, giving basis (1,0,−1,0),(0,1,0,−1) and dimension 2.')])
add(30,'Linear Algebra',r'For A=[[29,0,55,17],[1,28,46,26],[17,13,33,38],[21,67,0,13]], what is its largest real eigenvalue?',['101','67','103','113'],'A',['Inspect the row sums.','Use the maximum component of an eigenvector to bound the modulus of every eigenvalue.'],r'''Every row sums to 101, so A(1,1,1,1)ᵀ=101(1,1,1,1)ᵀ and 101 is an eigenvalue. For any complex eigenpair Av=λv choose i with |vᵢ|=maxⱼ|vⱼ|>0. Since entries are nonnegative, |λ||vᵢ|≤Σⱼaᵢⱼ|vⱼ|≤101|vᵢ|. Thus |λ|≤101. In particular no real eigenvalue exceeds 101, making it the largest real eigenvalue.''',r'''An eigenvalue λ of a square matrix A is a scalar for which Av=λv for some nonzero vector v. Constant row sum s makes the all-ones vector an eigenvector with eigenvalue s. The induced infinity norm of a matrix is the maximum absolute row sum, and every eigenvalue has modulus at most any induced matrix norm. One proof selects a component of an eigenvector of maximal magnitude and applies the triangle inequality to that row of the eigenvalue equation. For matrices with nonnegative entries and constant row sum s, the absolute row sum is s, so s is both an eigenvalue and the spectral radius. Nonnegativity is necessary for this particular bound from ordinary row sums; signed entries may have larger absolute row sums and larger eigenvalues.''',[('Find the largest real eigenvalue of [[2,3],[4,1]].','Rows sum to 5, giving eigenvalue 5. The nonnegative row-sum bound puts every eigenvalue within |λ|≤5, so 5 is largest. The other eigenvalue is −2.'),('Does row sum 1 imply spectral radius 1 for [[3,−2],[−2,3]]?','No. Eigenvalues are 1 on (1,1) and 5 on (1,−1). Negative entries invalidate the nonnegative row-sum bound; the absolute row sum is 5.')])
add(31,'Linear Algebra',r'''For n≥1 define fₙ∈C[0,π] by sin(nx)/sin x for 0<x<π, fₙ(0)=n, fₙ(π)=(−1)ⁿ⁻¹n. Let V=spanℝ{f₁,f₂,f₃}, with ⟨f,g⟩=(2/π)∫₀^π f(x)g(x)sin²x dx. Which is true?''',[r'f₄∈V.',r'{f₁,f₂,f₃} is an orthonormal basis of V.',r'dim V=2.',r'{f₁,f₂,f₃} is orthogonal but not orthonormal.'],'B',['The weight sin²x cancels the denominators in the products.','Use sine-function orthogonality on [0,π].'],r'''For positive m,n, ⟨fₘ,fₙ⟩=(2/π)∫₀^π sin(mx)sin(nx)dx=δₘₙ. This follows by the product-to-sum formula, with integral π/2 when m=n and zero otherwise. Thus f₁,f₂,f₃ are orthonormal, hence independent and a basis of their span, of dimension 3. The same identity gives f₄ orthogonal to V with norm 1; if f₄ belonged to V it would be orthogonal to itself, impossible. Only B is true.''',r'''An inner product is a positive-definite symmetric bilinear form on a real vector space. A family is orthogonal if distinct vectors have zero inner product and orthonormal if each vector also has norm one. Any finite orthogonal family of nonzero vectors is linearly independent: take inner products with each member in a proposed zero combination to obtain its coefficient. Weighted integral inner products ∫fgw are positive definite on continuous functions when the nonnegative weight is positive throughout the interval interior. Isolated endpoint zeros do not affect positivity. Sine functions on [0,π] satisfy ∫sin(mx)sin(nx)=0 for distinct positive integers and π/2 for equal indices, proved through product-to-sum identities. Scaling by 2/π normalizes their norms. Boundary extensions affect continuity, but endpoints do not change the integral.''',[('Normalize sin x under the ordinary L² inner product on [0,π].','Its squared norm is ∫sin²x dx=π/2, so √(2/π)sin x has norm one.'),('If u,v are orthogonal and have squared norms 2 and 3, find ‖2u−v‖².','Expanding the inner product gives 4‖u‖²−4⟨u,v⟩+‖v‖²=8+0+3=11.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
